What:           /dev/mt6797-imsc
Date:           October 2026
Contact:        Linux WWAN maintainers
Description:
                Exclusive MT6797 IMS control transport. The root-only (0600)
                miscellaneous device carries opaque IMCB records on native
                CCCI TX54/RX55, queue 6. It implements no IMS policy, automatic
                response, SIM authentication or registration operation.

                Each write supplies exactly 3456 bytes. The driver adds its
                own 16-byte CCCI data header and native transport sequence.
                Each read returns one complete payload, without that header;
                accepted RX records contain 64 through 3456 payload bytes.
                A short read buffer returns EMSGSIZE without consuming the
                record. A failed userspace copy retains the record unless the
                endpoint has become terminal. Unknown IMCB events remain opaque.

                Only one file owner is allowed (EBUSY for another open).
                Before modem READY, open returns EAGAIN. Nonblocking reads or
                writes return EAGAIN when no record or TX descriptor is ready.
                Blocking operations are interruptible; poll reports record/TX
                readiness and terminal ERR/HUP. A successful write means the
                complete record was published, even if STOP raced its return.
                It does not mean the modem completed the protocol operation.

                RX is limited to 32 records and 256 KiB of charged SKB memory,
                including a record detached for a userspace copy. Overflow,
                invalid endpoint framing or an RX55 record on another queue
                permanently stops this endpoint. Existing common CCCI header,
                sequence and modem-fatal policies run before endpoint dispatch
                and retain their wider transport scope.

                Loss of READY, native teardown or protocol abandonment is
                terminal. Closing after any published TX, delivered RX or
                queued RX requires a real modem restart before another owner
                can communicate. Empty untouched open/close remains reusable.
                There is no ioctl, synthetic reconnect, error-clear or reset
                command. This prevents a replacement IMCB owner from inheriting
                an earlier owner's uncertain outstanding operations. A future
                reusable handover requires the actual firmware disconnect
                contract, rather than reopening the file to discard state.

                No service is started by opening or registering this device.
